\ Benchmark: what the builtin-pair override guard costs compiled arithmetic.
\
\ Compiled code inlines `+ - * / % = < >` and `abs` on a fixnum or float pair behind a test of one
\ process-global bit, so that a `method{` arm replacing that pair is honored. A site whose operands
\ are known numbers also boxes its inline result, so a chain of arithmetic on known numbers stops
\ staying unboxed between operations. This file registers no arm, so every bit reads clear and what
\ it measures is the test and the boxing on the path every program takes.
\
\ Each loop is one self-tail-recursive word, so the whole iteration is one compiled body. The typed
\ loops carry known operands, which used to stay unboxed from one operation to the next. The untyped
\ loop's operand used to unbox once at its first operation and stay unboxed after it. Both shapes now
\ store each result to its slot and test its tag again at the next operation.
\
\ Run with --compile=eager or --compile=hybrid. Under --compile=off nothing here is compiled, so the
\ same file run that way is the layout control for an A/B of two builds.

iters: ( -- fixnum ) const [ 3000000 ] ;

fixnum-typed: ( acc: fixnum n: fixnum -- fixnum ) [
  dup 0 = [ drop ] [
    swap 3 * 7 + 1000003 % swap 1 - fixnum-typed
  ] if
] ;

fixnum-untyped: ( acc n -- acc ) [
  dup 0 = [ drop ] [
    swap 3 * 7 + 1000003 % swap 1 - fixnum-untyped
  ] if
] ;

float-typed: ( acc: float n: fixnum -- float ) [
  dup 0 = [ drop ] [
    swap 1.5 * 0.25 + dup 1000.0 > [ 1000.0 / ] when swap 1 - float-typed
  ] if
] ;

\ `benchmark` adds bookkeeping per word call, and a self-tail call compiles to a back-edge rather
\ than a call, so it times the loop without reaching inside it.
"fixnum-typed elapsed_ns: " print
[ 1 iters fixnum-typed drop ] benchmark "elapsed_ns" @get .

"fixnum-untyped elapsed_ns: " print
[ 1 iters fixnum-untyped drop ] benchmark "elapsed_ns" @get .

"float-typed elapsed_ns: " print
[ 1.0 iters float-typed drop ] benchmark "elapsed_ns" @get .
